      "status": "COMPLETED",
      "content": "## Summary of verified results (turn limit reached)\n\n### Completed work\n\n**1. Handbook extraction & key definitions (verified from `workspace/data/handbook.pdf`, extracted to `temp/handbook.txt`, 24 pages):**\n\n| Item | Verified value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Preheat temperature region | 100°C – 150°C | \"In the preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT assembly to temperatures generally between 100°C to 150°C\" |\n| Ramp rate limit | rise rate < **2°C/second** | \"The most critical parameter in the preheat section is to minimize the rate of rise to less than 2°C/second\" |\n| Solder melting point (63/37 eutectic) | 183°C | \"For 63/37 Eutectic solder, the melting temperature is 183°C\" |\n| Peak requirement | liquidus + **~20°C** | \"This temperature must be exceeded by approximately 20°C to ensure quality reflow\" |\n| Wetting time (TAL) window | **30 – 60 seconds** | \"The wetting time is normally 30 to 60 seconds for most pastes\" |\n| Line speed (minimum) formula | `boards_per_min × board_length / load_factor` | \"LINE SPEED (MINIMUM) = BOARDS PER MINUTE × LENGTH PER BOARD / LOAD FACTOR\" |\n| Process speed formula | `oven chamber heated length / process dwell time` | \"PROCESS SPEED = OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH / PROCESS DWELL TIME\" |\n| Feasibility rule | actual conveyor speed must be **≥** minimum line speed | \"Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated speed ensures that no product jams will occur\" |\n\n**2. Data structure verified (all 4 files under `workspace/data/`):**\n- `mes_log.csv`: 60 runs (R0001–R0060), 6 board families (Compute_IO, Industrial_CTRL, Mobile_PWR, RF_FrontEnd, Sensor_Module, Wearable). Fields include `conveyor_speed_cm_min` (37.1–106.7), `board_length_cm` (constant per family: 30/32/25/18/20/15), `loading_factor` (0.17–0.95, per run), `boards_per_min` (per run), `solder_liquidus_c` (values: 217, 227, 228, 229).\n- `thermocouples.csv`: 6 TCs per run (TC1–TC6, distinct locations), 12 zones, time/temperature traces.\n- `test_defects.csv`: 60 runs; each run has exactly one `SUMMARY` row with `fp_yield_pct`, `units_tested`, `units_pass`, `units_rework`, `units_scrap`, `count`, plus per-inspection-stage defect rows.\n\n**3. Skill loaded:** `reflow-profile-compliance-toolkit` — provides deterministic computation recipes (finite-difference max slope in temp band with both-endpoints filtering, interpolated time-above-threshold, tie-breaks: max-metric → highest value then smallest tc_id; min/coldest-metric → lowest value then smallest tc_id; 2-dp rounding; sorted output; null for missing data).\n\n### Incomplete work\n\n**No output files have been created yet** — `workspace/output/q01.json` through `q05.json` do not exist, and no calculations have been executed. Remaining steps:\n\n1. **Q1** — compute max preheat ramp per run (temp band 100–150°C, limit 2.0 C/s), select representative TC (max ramp, tie → smallest tc_id), list violating runs.\n2. **Q2** — compute TAL per run using each run's `solder_liquidus_c` (interpolated), window 30–60 s, per-TC results with compliant/non-compliant status.\n3. **Q3** — peak per run (coldest TC = min of per-TC peaks, tie → smallest tc_id), required peak = liquidus + 20°C; runs without TC data → failing.\n4. **Q4** — `required_min_speed = boards_per_min × board_length_cm / loading_factor` per run, compare to actual `conveyor_speed_cm_min`. Open item: the spec sheet's PROCESS SPEED table (heated length / dwell time) is garbled in extraction and the data's `oven_model` values (8Z_Convection, 10Z_Convection, 8Z_IR+Conv) do not map cleanly to spec sheet model names (4470MP/XP/HP/HN, 4480XG, 4470XN), so the \"allowable conveyor speed\" constraint could not be verified from the spec sheet.\n5. **Q5** — best run per board_family by quality + efficiency. The handbook contains **no explicit run-selection rule**; a deterministic criterion (e.g., highest `fp_yield_pct` from SUMMARY, tie-break by throughput/conveyor speed, then run_id) would need to be defined and applied.\n\nNo hallucinated values were introduced; all numbers above are directly from the handbook text or the data files.",
